course: dictionary
lesson: 7350
title: Plausibility: does it sound believable?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Plausibility is a noun. It tells you how believable or likely to be true something seems. A plausible story could really happen, and an implausible one seems unlikely or impossible.
explain: In a text, read the sentence and ask: does this sound believable? Look at the example: «cheek and plausibility such as yours, combined with the power of a long purse». A long purse means lots of money, so the sentence suggests someone can sound convincing and back it up with money, which is what makes their talk hard to resist.
explain: In maths, plausibility helps you catch mistakes. If 8 times 9 gives 700, the answer is not plausible, because it should be about 70. Always ask: is this answer about the right size?
explain: In science and media, a claim can sound plausible and still be false. Check where the information came from and whether trusted sources agree before you believe it.
fact: Plausibility means how believable or likely to be true something seems.
fact: «Plausible» is the adjective: a plausible story sounds like it could be true.
fact: Something can seem plausible and still be false, so you should check.
fact: In maths, checking plausibility means asking whether an answer is about the right size.
fact: Plausibility is about how something seems, not proof that it is true.
exercise: What does plausibility mean? | How long something lasts | *How believable something seems | How many words a sentence has | How loud a sound is
exercise: Sam says 8 times 9 is 700. Is this answer plausible? | Yes, because Sam worked very hard on it | Yes, because it is a big number | *No, 8 times 9 is about 70, so 700 is far too big
exercise: In «cheek and plausibility such as yours, combined with the power of a long purse», what is a long purse? | A very long stick | *A lot of money | A very big bag | A long walk to the shops
test: An advert says, «Eat this and grow ten centimetres by Friday!» Is the claim plausible? | *No, nobody grows that fast, so it is not plausible | Yes, adverts are always true | Yes, because it is written in big letters
test: You read a shocking claim online. What is a good check on how plausible it is? | Believe it, because it was written very well | Share it at once, because it sounds sure of itself | *See where it came from and whether trusted sources agree
exam: In «cheek and plausibility such as yours», what does plausibility mean? | How much food was eaten | How tall someone is | How fast the wind blows | *How believable the talk sounds
